# ONE TAG, PLUS ONE FILE THAT IS NOT IN IT.
#
# ── THE RECEIPT-VERIFIER CORE IS PINNED TO A RELEASED TAG: v1.2.1 ───────────────────────────
#
# The shared verification core here comes from receipt-verifier **v1.2.1**, peeled commit
# e1a346bcb62532a9151cfe180b5ac2a33786d9a1.
#
# MEASURED 2026-11-06, every pinned entry against `git show v1.2.1:<path>` in receipt-verifier:
#   the TWELVE .js files below — verify, arity, verify-bundle, verify-grant, verify-attest,
#   verify-toolset, verify-atomic-attestation, evidence-root, verify-evidence,
#   verified-execution-binding, verify-prove-transcript, receipt-from-commit — are ALL
#   byte-identical to the tag.
#
# Hash lines that moved from v1.0.3: verify.js and verify-grant.js, over the receipt-verifier path
# (first tag of each commit, measured): 1.1.1 P66 v<4 is not authorization, a non-string signed field
# is MALFORMED; 1.1.4 a receipt beside an envelope must be v4; 1.1.5 Ed25519 only; 1.1.6 --as-of,
# expiry applies under a retired key; 1.1.7 strict JSON parse, and grants (verify-grant.js: revoked_at
# refuses a grant at every instant, strict grant bodies); 1.1.8 --as-of before ts is
# NOT_YET_VALID_AT_AS_OF; 1.2.0 the RECEIPT_FORMAT §7.1 key-status order. Every other hash line is
# unchanged because those files are byte-identical to v1.0.3.
#
# The one genuine exception, and it keeps its own note because it is still true:
#   xlang      = this tree's copy of the generator output (806ab018, regenerated 2026-11-06 with
#                receipt-verifier scripts/vectors/gen-xlang-vectors.js). The file is
#                NOT in receipt-verifier (ephemeral-key generator). Hashing a
#                missing receipt-verifier/xlang-vectors.json is empty-string sha
#                e3b0c442 — that is not a pin of a real corpus.
#
# Before v1.0.0 the core was pinned to a WORKING TREE and to per-file commits. Both are honest about
# where the bytes came from and neither is a provenance a stranger can resolve. A released tag is a
# name anyone can check out — that is the difference between a pin and a note.
#
#
# ── AND THE TAG IS SIGNED, WHICH IS THE PART A STRANGER CAN CHECK ───────────────────────────
#
#   tag object   e6dd547d445c6b22627a2da8a4946165b62affe6
#   peeled commit e1a346bcb62532a9151cfe180b5ac2a33786d9a1
#   signer       zsobpeter@gmail.com
#   fingerprint  SHA256:7yRXTm9zKGicfFpzL+7lpwFoPaoSwxAJlabB3jwxw2Y
#
# Verify it yourself, in a checkout of receipt-verifier:
#
#   git tag -v v1.2.1
#   -> Good "git" signature for zsobpeter@gmail.com with ED25519 key SHA256:7yRXTm9zKGicfFpzL+7lpwFoPaoSwxAJlabB3jwxw2Y
#
# Previous pin: v1.0.3 (tag object bb5753681275920e7e23351c6b41632957e6238f, peeled 523e0a2, same signer).
#
# source_commit: e1a346bcb62532a9151cfe180b5ac2a33786d9a1 (receipt-verifier v1.2.1, signed)
#

